  "statement": "Find a closed form for $S_{n}=\\sum_{k=1}^{n}k\\,2^{k}$ and verify it for $n=1,2,3$.",
  "solution": "Let $S=2+2\\cdot 2^{2}+3\\cdot 2^{3}+\\cdots+n2^{n}$. Then $2S=2^{2}+2\\cdot 2^{3}+\\cdots+(n-1)2^{n}+n2^{n+1}$. Subtracting, $S-2S=2+2^{2}+2^{3}+\\cdots+2^{n}-n2^{n+1}$, so $-S=(2^{n+1}-2)-n2^{n+1}$. Thus $S=2+(n-1)2^{n+1}$. Checks: $n=1\\Rightarrow 2$; $n=2\\Rightarrow 2+8=10$; $n=3\\Rightarrow 2+32=34$.",
  "hints": [
    "Write $S$ and $2S$ and subtract to telescope.",
    "Use the geometric sum for the remaining powers of 2."
  ],
